## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical module design and basic policy; it checks input validation, derived values, and CI gates.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform module input validation
- Local/derived values
- Regex-based naming conventions
- Basic GitHub Actions gates

## Code Example
```terraform
variable "env" {
  type = string
  description = "Environment: dev|staging|prod"
  validation {
    condition = contains(["dev","staging","prod"], var.env)
    error_message = "env must be dev, staging, or prod"
  }
}

variable "bucket_name" {
  type    = string
  default = ""
}

resource "random_id" "suffix" { byte_length = 4 }

locals {
  derived_bucket = var.bucket_name != "" ? var.bucket_name : "${var.env}-app-${random_id.suffix.hex}"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bucket" {
  bucket = local.derived_bucket
  versioning {
    enabled = true
  }
  server_side_encryption_configuration {
    rule {
      apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
        sse_algorithm = "AES256"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you integrate this with a per-env backend and a separate plan stage for prod-only changes?
- How would you extend validation to enforce unique bucket names across multiple regions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of multi-account Terraform setups with minimal code duplication, demonstrating proficiency with provider aliases, wrapper modules, and CI gates to enforce environment boundaries.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliases for multi-account AWS access
- Backend/workspace separation for state isolation
- Wrapper modules for environment context management
- CI gates to enforce production change protections

## Code Example
```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.0"
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "terraform-state-bucket"
    key    = "${terraform.workspace}/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "prod"
  region = "us-east-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "sandbox"
  region = "us-east-1"
}

module "environment" {
  source = "./modules/environment-wrapper"
  providers = {
    aws = terraform.workspace == "prod" ? aws.prod : aws.sandbox
  }
  environment = terraform.workspace
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a GitOps, multi-cloud per-PR sandbox architecture with isolated backends, secret management, and policy gates. It also probes automation for ephemeral environments and teardown reliability.

## Key Concepts
- Per-PR sandbox architecture across AWS, Azure, GCP
- Provider aliases per cloud and per PR
- Per-PR remote backends and state isolation
- Vault-backed credentials with rotation
- Encryption at rest and in transit
- Policy-as-code gates (OPA/Sentinel) in CI
- Teardown automation on PR lifecycle

## Code Example
```hcl
# Minimal scaffolding
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = { source = "hashicorp/aws" }
    azurerm = { source = "hashicorp/azurerm" }
    google = { source = "hashicorp/google" }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "pr1"
  region = "us-east-1"
}
provider "azurerm" {
  alias    = "pr1"
  features = {}
}
provider "google" {
  alias   = "pr1"
  project = "pr-12345"
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement per-PR teardown if resources are tainted or fail during apply?
- What strategies ensure Vault rotation never disrupts in-flight deployments while keeping state secure?
